course: adult-life
lesson: 30
title: Police, courts and prison
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every country has laws, rules everyone must follow, and people whose job is to keep them: the police catch and question suspects, courts decide fairly whether someone broke the law, and prison is the punishment for the most serious crimes.
explain: The most important idea is fairness: a person is innocent until proven guilty, and both sides get to tell their story with evidence.
explain: Picture a week in Birmingham: a speeding ticket arrives with a photo of Dad's car, Mum gets a letter saying she must do jury service, and the neighbours are burgled while they are on holiday.
explain: Crime is on the news a lot, but most people live their whole lives without serious crime touching them.
explain: Police officers are people you can go to for help if you are lost or scared.
fact: A siren and flashing blue lights mean police are rushing to an emergency, and other drivers must pull over to let them through.
fact: Police can stop drivers to check things, such as a broken light or speeding; it is usually short and may end with a warning or a fine, and it is not the same as being arrested.
fact: 999 is the emergency number in the UK (112 also works, and in the USA and Canada it is 911); it is for real emergencies only, and prank calls block the line.
fact: Being arrested means the police think someone may have committed a crime and will question them; it is not yet proof.
fact: A burglary is when someone breaks into a home to steal; victims are often upset most by feeling unsafe, and insurance can pay for some of what was taken.
fact: A court is where it is decided, under strict rules, whether someone broke the law and what should happen; most trials are open to the public.
fact: A judge is an expert in the law who runs a court case fairly and decides the punishment; British judges' wigs and robes are an old tradition.
fact: A jury is twelve ordinary adults picked at random who listen to a criminal trial and decide whether the person is guilty or not guilty.
fact: A solicitor is a lawyer who gives advice and writes legal papers; a barrister argues cases in court; getting a lawyer does not mean someone did wrong.
fact: "Sent down" is slang for being sent to prison; prison punishes, protects the public and is meant to help people change.
fact: A speed camera photographs cars going over the limit, and the driver must pay a fine and may get points on their licence.
fact: Speed limits exist because faster cars cause worse accidents, especially near schools and homes.
fact: "Innocent until proven guilty" means a person is treated as innocent until a court proves, with evidence, that they did the crime.
exercise: A police car races past with its siren on and blue lights flashing. What is happening? | Police are rushing to an emergency | Police are chasing you for something | Police are testing their lights for fun | Police are late for their lunch break
exercise: What must drivers do when they hear a siren behind them? | Pull over to let it through | Speed up to race it | Stop in the middle of the road | Turn on their own siren
exercise: A police officer pulls Mum over: "Do you know why I've stopped you, madam?" Is Mum being arrested? | No, it is a check that may end with a warning or a fine | Yes, she is going straight to prison | Yes, all stopped drivers are arrested and taken to the police station | No, the officer just wants directions
exercise: At school: "In an emergency, call 999." When should you call it? | When someone is badly hurt, there is a fire or a crime | When you are bored at home and want somebody to chat to | When you lose your homework | When the TV stops working
exercise: What number do you call in an emergency in the USA? | 911 | 999 | 111 | 101
exercise: The TV news: "A man has been arrested following a stabbing." Does that prove he did it? | No, he will be questioned; it is not yet proof | Yes, arrested people are always guilty of what they did | Yes, the news has already decided | No, because arrests are only jokes
exercise: Neighbour: "We've been burgled — they took the laptop and Mum's ring." What happened? | Someone broke into their home and stole things | They left the laptop and the ring on a train by mistake | They gave the ring to a friend | A storm blew their things away
exercise: What can help pay for things stolen in a burglary? | Insurance | The school | The neighbours | The lottery
exercise: On the news: "He appeared at the Crown Court today." What is a court? | The place where it is decided if someone broke the law | The place where kings and queens live | The place where people play tennis | The place where prisoners sleep at night in their cells
exercise: A picture shows a judge in a red robe and a curly white wig. What does a judge do? | Runs a court case fairly and decides the punishment | Arrests people in the street | Argues for one side only, like a lawyer for the accused | Guards the prisoners in jail
exercise: Mum opens a letter: "Oh no, I've got jury service." What will she do? | Sit on a jury of twelve and decide guilty or not guilty | Work as a police officer for a week, helping with arrests | Become a judge for a month | Go to prison to visit someone
exercise: How are jury members chosen? | At random from ordinary adults | By the judge's friends | Only from police officers | By who wins a vote
exercise: Grown-up: "We'll have to get a solicitor involved." What is a solicitor? | A lawyer who gives legal advice and writes papers | A police officer who arrests people and takes them to the cells | A doctor who signs sick notes | A teacher who marks exams
exercise: Does getting a solicitor mean someone did something wrong? | No, it often means they want things done properly | Yes, only people who have done a crime ever need solicitors | Yes, solicitors work for the prison | No, solicitors only sell houses
exercise: Overheard: "He got sent down for five years." What does it mean? | He was sent to prison for five years | He moved to a flat downstairs for five years | He was sent to work in a mine for five years | He was demoted at work for five years
exercise: Dad opens a letter with a photo of his car: "A speeding fine! Sixty quid!" What happened? | A speed camera caught his car going over the limit | Someone stole his car and sold it | He won sixty pounds in a photo competition in the local paper | He parked his car in the wrong place
exercise: Why are there speed limits near schools? | Faster cars cause worse accidents | Cars use less petrol slowly | Teachers do not like noise | Children like watching cars
exercise: A reporter says: "He denies the charges and is innocent until proven guilty." What does that mean? | He is treated as innocent until a court proves he did it | He is definitely innocent | He has already been found guilty by the police who arrested him | He can never be put on trial
test: Your brother: "Mum got pulled over, so she's a criminal now!" Put him right. | No, being stopped is a check, not an arrest | Yes, anyone stopped by police is a criminal | Yes, she will go to court tomorrow | No, because mums cannot be stopped
test: Your mate dials 999 "for a laugh" and hangs up. Why is that serious? | It blocks the line for people in real danger | It costs the police lots of petrol | It is fine as long as you hang up fast | It is only serious after midnight
test: Neighbour, shaking: "It's not the telly I care about, it's knowing someone was in our bedroom." Why is she upset? | Burglary makes people feel unsafe at home | She did not like the telly much anyway | She is cross the burglar was untidy | She wants a new bedroom
test: Kids in the playground: "Jake took my rubber, everyone says so, so he gets detention." What would a fair court do? | Hear both sides and look at the evidence first | Punish Jake because everyone in the class already says he did it | Punish the one who shouts the loudest | Ask the tallest pupil to decide
test: Mum, after two weeks of jury service: "Can't tell you a thing about it, love." Why? | Jurors must keep the case private | She has forgotten everything already | It was too boring to talk about | She was not really on a jury
test: Gossip: "Gary's brother's been sent down, three years, his poor mum." What should you do if you see Gary? | Be kind, because it is hard for his family | Tease Gary about his brother | Tell everyone Gary is a criminal too | Stop talking to Gary for ever
test: Dad, fuming: "Sixty quid and three points, for doing 36 in a bloody 30!" What did he do? | He drove faster than the speed limit | He parked on the double yellow lines | He drove through a red light | He forgot to pay for petrol
test: On the news: "A woman has been arrested on suspicion of fraud." Your sister: "So she did it." What is right? | Not yet; a court must decide with evidence | Yes, an arrest proves she did it | Yes, the reporter says so | No, she is definitely innocent
test: A judge in a wig gives a punishment. A jury said "guilty". Who decided the punishment? | The judge | The jury | The police | The victim
test: You are lost in a big shopping centre. Who is a good person to go to? | A police officer or a member of staff | A stranger in the car park | Nobody, just keep walking | The first person who calls you over
test: Overheard: "Sod the police, if I find who nicked my bike I'll sort them myself." Why do we have courts instead? | So punishment is fair, not revenge | So people can take revenge themselves | So the bike is returned faster | So nobody ever gets punished
exam: Identify the principle in accordance with which a person accused of a crime is treated prior to a finding of guilt. | That the person is innocent until proven guilty | That the person is guilty until proven innocent | That the person is guilty if arrested | That the person may be punished by the public
exam: It is given that an adult has received a summons to perform jury service. Ascertain the function the adult will perform. | To decide, with eleven others, whether an accused person is guilty | To pass sentence upon a convicted person | To arrest suspects within the court building | To argue on behalf of the accused person and to cross-examine witnesses
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises the expression "sent down" as employed in respect of a convicted person. | Committed to prison | Relocated to a lower floor | Demoted in employment | Released without charge
exam: Determine the principal reason for which speed limits are imposed in the vicinity of schools and dwellings. | Higher speeds cause more serious accidents | Lower speeds reduce the cost of fuel | Teachers object to the noise of traffic | Speed cameras require slow-moving vehicles
